		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=3383#p3383"><![CDATA[
Hi David,<br><br>Quasi-Newton preconditioner, Conjugate Gradient solver and then non-linear line search.  Would be an interesting exercise to figure out how to do that efficiently.  Projected Gausse Seidel implemented with an accumulation vector seems to work pretty well.  The accumulator gives you a LCP solver that is linear in processing time and memory.  If I took your recipe and implemented your technique I would end up with a system that is O(n^3) cpu time and O(n^2) in memory needed.  Which is what "LCP solver" used to be.  Is your QN/CG/NLS implementation also linear in time/space requirements similar to PGS with an accumulator ( aka sequential impulses )?<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=323">mewert</a> — Tue Jan 30, 2007 3:04 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
